---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<Base title="The journey" description="How OpenJevX went from a 77.4% fine-tune to a stable, calibrated model, told as a changelog.">
  <h1>The journey</h1>
  <p class="lead">A plain engineering changelog, not a highlight reel. Every number below is
    quoted from a report or file in this repo — sources are named as we go.</p>

  <div class="timeline">
    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="tl-card">
        <div class="tl-tag">v0.1 — where we started</div>
        <h3>Laya, fine-tuned on public data</h3>
        <p>v0.1 was Laya (<code>convaiinnovations/laya</code>) fine-tuned on the public{' '}
          <code>typed-decisions</code> dataset. That fine-tune scored <strong>77.4%</strong> on its own
          benchmark, measured on PyTorch with a GPU
          (<a href={`${base}/results/`}>see the quantization table</a>,
          source: <code>clauderesults/01-quantization-and-everyday-domain-report.md</code>).</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="tl-card">
        <div class="tl-tag">v0.2 — what broke</div>
        <h3>An int8 bug, and a near-chance everyday model</h3>
        <p>Two separate problems showed up once the shipped file was actually tested, not just the
          training checkpoint (<code>clauderesults/01-quantization-and-everyday-domain-report.md</code>):</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The shipped int8 file lost ~12 points.</strong> ADR 0003 had called int8 quantization
            "no material quality loss" based on a PyTorch/GPU number. On the actual shipped ONNX file,
            the public test dropped to <strong>65.1%</strong> vs. <strong>76.8%</strong> for fp32 on the
            same weights. Root cause: dynamic int8 quantizes activations per batch, so the same question
            could get a different answer depending on what else was in the request — verified directly,
            the same 6 yes/no questions asked alone vs. in a batch of 6 gave <strong>0 of 6</strong>
            matching answers on int8.</li>
          <li><strong>On everyday software decisions, the model was near chance.</strong> On a 5,200-case
            rule-labelled everyday set (20 domains: incidents, refunds, invoices, fraud, deploys,
            security, and others), even fp32 landed at <strong>46.8%</strong> — 30% on score questions,
            38% on choice questions. Yes/no answers largely ignored the state: stock at 0 units scored
            0.117 vs. 0.102 at 5,000 units for "reorder now"; checkout at 95% errors scored 0.15 vs. 0.07
            at 0% errors for "urgent." Through jevx's own default thresholds, most obvious answers came
            back "unsure" — confidence around 0.59–0.68 on facts like "stock is 0, is it out of stock,"
            below jevx's yes ≥ 0.8 bar (<code>clauderesults/02-v0.3.0-release-and-jevx-report.md</code>).</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="tl-card">
        <div class="tl-tag">v0.3 — 4-bit weight-only</div>
        <h3>Fixing the quantization, no retrain needed</h3>
        <p>Switched to a 4-bit weight-only ONNX graph (<code>MatMulNBits</code>, block 32, symmetric):
          weights are quantized, activations stay float, so the answer no longer depends on batch
          composition. Result: <strong>77.0%</strong> on the public test at 461 MB, vs. 65.1% for the
          retired int8 at 598 MB. An 8-bit weight-only variant was also built for comparison
          (76.9%, 598 MB, slower) — not shipped, since 4-bit was equally accurate and smaller
          (<code>clauderesults/02-v0.3.0-release-and-jevx-report.md</code>).</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="tl-item">
      <div class="tl-dot"></div>
      <div class="tl-card">
        <div class="tl-tag">v0.4 — fine-tune on real work</div>
        <h3>443,394 software-role decisions, $1.80 to train</h3>
        <p>Trained on 443,394 decisions: 183,543 rule-labelled software-role decisions across 56
          families and 7 roles (ops, developers, testers, leads, managers, and agent-style checks like
          claim verification and ticket routing), plus a stratified sample of the original{' '}
          <code>train_openjevx</code> data and the typed-decisions set oversampled 4x
          (<code>clauderesults/08-morning-report.md</code>, <code>.local/release/v0.4.0/benchmark.json</code>).</p>

        <p>Before training, <strong>22,576 leaked test questions</strong> were found and removed —{' '}
          <code>train_openjevx.jsonl</code> contained 18,269 questions that also appeared in the{' '}
          <code>eval_openjevx</code> test set, 8,372 from the opencode test set, 972 from the owner's own
          CSV, and all 1,194 rule-set questions. Every number in the results table is measured after that
          cleanup, so it's honest about generalisation, not memorisation.</p>

        <p>Quality judges (NLI DeBERTa and Selene-1-Mini locally, plus free OpenRouter models) were
          calibrated against a trusted key before being allowed near the data. None reached the 90%
          accuracy bar required to trust a verdict — the best, Selene on yes/no questions, topped out at
          73% — so per the standing rule, <strong>no judge was allowed to delete or flag any row</strong>.
          Zero rows were removed by any automated judge; the fine-tune data quality reports
          (<code>clauderesults/06-data-quality-report.md</code>, <code>clauderesults/07-local-quality-report.md</code>)
          document why and leave every source formally "unverified," not "bad."</p>

        <p>Training ran on a single rented RTX 4090 (vast.ai): a $0.27 smoke test, then a $1.53 full run
          capped at a 2.75-hour budget (~90% of one full pass over the data, ~40 decisions/second).
          <strong>Total cost: $1.80.</strong> Both boxes were destroyed after; no training data was left on
          any box or bucket.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Results, before → after</h2>
  <p>See the full table on the <a href={`${base}/results/`}>before/after page</a>. Headline:
    software-role held-out accuracy went from <strong>63.7% → 98.1%</strong>, and previously-unseen
    software-role families went from <strong>66.1% → 87.3%</strong>. The public typed-decisions test
    barely moved: <strong>76.1% → 76.2%</strong>.</p>

  <div class="note">
    <strong>Why 80% on the public test isn't reachable by more training.</strong> Where the human
    labellers who built that test agreed with each other, every model version already scores
    ~87–89%. Where they disagreed (41% of the test), every version scores ~60%, because the
    "right" answer there is a split vote among humans. Hitting 80% overall would require ~68%
    accuracy on questions the labellers themselves couldn't agree on — the original
    typed-decisions-only fine-tune topped out at 76.8% for the same structural reason. This is a
    ceiling in the public test's labelling, not a training gap
    (<code>clauderesults/08-morning-report.md</code>).
  </div>

  <p>The v0.4.0 release was <strong>staged, not auto-published</strong>, because the public test
    stayed under the owner's 80% publish bar; it shipped once the owner reviewed the full picture
    above and judged the software-role and calibration gains worth it on their own terms.</p>

  <p><a href={`${base}/results/`}>See the numbers →</a></p>
</Base>
